   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend the arts and cultural affairs law, in relation to renaming\nagency building one, agency building two, agency building three and agency\nbuilding four, located at the empire state plaza",
    "basePrintNo": "S9685",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S9685-2025",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [],
     "size": 0
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Amd §3.05, Arts & Cul L",
    "lawSection": "Arts and Cultural Affairs Law",
    "memo": " \nBILL NUMBER: S9685\n\nSPONSOR: FAHY\n \nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act to amend the arts and cultural affairs law, in relation to renam-\ning agency building one, agency building two, agency building three and\nagency building four, located at the empire state plaza\n\n \nPURPOSE:\n\nTo rename agency buildings one through four at the Empire state plaza.\n\n \nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nSection 1 adds a new subdivision 12 to section 3.05 of the arts and\ncultural affairs law to provide a process for renaming agency building\none through four on the Empire State Plaza.\n\nSection 2 provides for the effective date which is one hundred days\nafter enactment.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nThe Governor Nelson A. Rockefeller Empire State Plaza is one of the most\nspectacular capital centers in the country. It contains major buildings\nlike the Egg Performance Center, Cultural Education Center as well as\noffice space for thousands of state employees Many of the Plaza build-\nings are named after famous people including Erastus Corning, the long-\nest tenured mayor of any large city the United States (Corning Tower)\nand Robert Abrams, a long time State Attorney General, (The Robert\nAbrams Building for Law and Justice). The four building that house thou-\nsands of state employees are named 1,2,3, and 4. The naming of building\nsimple numbers misses a great opportunity to educate New Yorkers. This\nlegislation honors New York's most significant natural resource and/or\nhistorical events.\n\nThis bill sets up a process for the selection of the names by using an\nexisting Council. The Council will recommend options to the Governor.\nShe will be the ultimate decision maker after a robust public engagement\nprocess. New Yorkers who the Capital complex visit will be reminded of\nthe great natural resources and historical events of the Empire State.\n\nThis bill attempts to tackle all the issues that Governor Hochul\nexpressed in her veto (58) of 2025.\n\n \nLEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\nA9059 of 2024, referred to tourism, parks, arts and sports development\nPage 1 of 2\n\nA1914 of 2025 - Veto 58 (of a similar bill)\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\n\nNone.\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nThis act shall take effect one hundred days after it shall have become a\nlaw.\n",
